it is great to be joined today by  Charles Eisenstein who's a noted  activists thinker and author his latest  book is climate a new story Charles it's  so great to talk to you I really  appreciate it yeah thanks for having me  on so to start with if you were to say  to me hey I've identified some problems  in how the left is dealing with climate  change I would kind of have two  different areas I would want to sort of  think about one of those areas would be  are those who believe the climate  science doing the wrong things in order  to try to mitigate the problem but then  the other side of it is is the way that  the issue is being communicated part of  the problem in terms of getting more  people to understand the reality of  what's going on let's maybe start there  are both of these issues is one more of  an issue than the other I think that  they are both issues and that they are  related to each other so the way that  the problem is being addressed and  considered on a policy level has some of  the same problems that the way that it's  being rejected that's being addressed on  a rhetorical level so the other book  related it so maybe we'll start with the  messaging part and then that will lead  us to maybe how the policy part is not  actually the the right approach so first  in terms of messaging what do you  identify that is being done incorrectly  by those who believe the science want to  deal with this issue but where are they  going wrong on the messaging well  there's a few a few levels of that I  think on a very basic level the  messaging that the essential messaging  that answers well why should we do  anything about the problem and answers  that if we don't bad things will happen  to us I think that invoking a fear  mentality that looks at nature for it's  for what it does for us that's part of  the problem to say they say well why  should we care about the rest of life  why should we care about the world  because bad things will happen to us  that denies that  that the rainforests the whales the  coral reefs the the soil the water has  inherent value in its own right and so  it contributes to the instrumentalizing  and distancing of nature that I think is  at the root of the problem to begin with  okay so let's deal with that in more  detail I agree with you my question is  at this point if you still either deny  the science or are uncaring about it or  whatever are you going to be more likely  to say wow we really do need to take  action now if we talk about the reefs  and other animals in other words aren't  these people already haven't they  already demonstrated they don't care  about that well I think that the people  who don't believe climate science and  and the general narrative of climate  change are adopting that partly as part  of a political identity that goes way  beyond climate change so if you are you  know a trump supporter and identify  generally with conservative political  beliefs then you're gonna be predisposed  to reject the climate narrative yes this  time you might really care about your  your local forests and streams and you  might not want to have your groundwater  your water table polluted with fracking  fluid and you might have places where  you love to hunt and fish  so by Framing it is primarily in terms  of this politicized global issue  sometimes we risk eliminating potential  allies who might really care about  localized ecocide and that leads to the  other part of my thesis which is that  localized ecocide is much more important  than then the carbon reductionist  narrative understands okay so in one  sense I mean I think part of what you're  saying is and correct me if I'm wrong  that when you hear about global ocean  temperature's going up by X amount or  these sort of very  at level figures that on the one hand  that can be hard to kind of understand  intellectually in terms of what it means  or it might not even sound like that  much what's one degree Celsius anyway  but that it also kind of misses the ways  in which individual and local  communities will also be affected is  that part of it making it local yeah so  like I was I'm in Rhode Island I'm here  with my wife where we where she grew up  and you know we're down on the in the  Narragansett Beach area she's like wow  that used to be a clamming beach right  there you know and these tidal pools  they used to have seein enemies and they  stabbed all kinds at least to see eels  in this estuary here and like all that  is gone and the seagrass Meadows like  eighty percent of them are gone from New  England and so people like there's like  she could talk to anybody around here  and they'd be like yeah that that that's  really too bad whether or not they  whatever their political orientation and  what we're what I'm seeing is that um  these are not only local issues like  seagrass Meadows for example sequester  enormous amounts of carbon kelp forests  with with lively with with robust  shellfish populations they sequester a  lot of carbon so even from a carbon  narrative I mean not to mention wetlands  forests and so forth ecosystems  contribute immensely to planetary  homeostasis and and climate equilibrium  and that's only thinking about carbon if  you go to the water cycle the  contribution toward climate stability of  ecosystems is even greater so it's kind  of like this you know do you care about  the local or do you care about the the  greenhouse gas levels it's kind of a  false dichotomy and and we've made that  a false dichotomy with the to some  extent at least with the polarizing  rhetoric and political positioning  around it that I think is not necessary  or ideal so let's let's take some of  those ideas and take them to the next  level let's assume we're dealing with  folks who  understand what you're saying about both  the local and and the planet-wide level  who either with more math or less math  understand what's going on and sort of  accept it then we often hear okay listen  I think that the free markets going to  solve it I think that science and  technology and industry will always be a  step ahead because their livelihood  depends on it if the stuff that you say  is going to happen happens all these  businesses literally will go out of  business I think that that has already  demonstrably failed and that's the  approach I usually take but how would  you deal with someone who tries to make  that case that it's all going to be  sorted out yeah there's two levels of  problem with that one well three one of  which is that it hasn't worked as you  point out secondly that in order for  markets to take ecological health  well-being into account the it has to be  monetized somehow so there has to be  some kind of metrics through which the  ecological costs can be internalized  onto the balance sheet so something like  the carbon tax or pollution taxes or  some kind of quota system for natural  resources things like that make  pollution expensive or emissions  expensive and so theoretically then you  could invoke market mechanisms to  incentivize pro-environmental behavior  but that leads to an even deeper level  of the problem which is that it assumes  that ecological health can be quantified  but what happens is when we try that we  end up quantifying only the things that  we are able to measure or that we think  are important or that  can be measured and it's one of the deep  conceits of our civilization that that  this is possible that we can some day  encompass the entire world in a set of  data but ordinarily usually what happens  when you try that is that the things  that we don't see  left out which actually then turn out to  be really important and and that the  metrics used encode the interests of the  people and institutions that have  created the metrics so you know what  gets left out who gets left out  something always gets left out like if  you're wanting to monetize Carbon  somehow then emissions are quite easy to  quantify above-ground biomass is sort of  easy to quantify below ground biomass  not so easy and what about the carbon  contribution of a whale what's that if  you if you use the obvious metrics it's  it's very close to zero but when you  understand that plummeting whale  populations deprived the orcas of food  so that they turn on the seals and the  sea otters and eat them which then no  longer keep the sea urchins in check  which then proliferate and destroy the  kelp forests which no longer feed the  shellfish which sequester carbon like  yeah and you're like oh I guess the  whales were pretty important after all  too bad we didn't include them in our  metrics so so I think that we need to  move beyond a metrics centred approach  to ecological health and therefore also  beyond just a market centred approach  yeah that's uh that's extraordinarily  depressing of an example it will  resonate with our audience okay since we  don't have unlimited time I want to  focus a little bit on in terms of the  policy and what which should actually be  done in the immediate like now through  the next five years what are the actual  low-hanging fruit and sort of like must  dues that the pro environment community  should be calling for and focusing on  yeah so number one priority is  conservation of all remaining pristine  ecosystems especially the amazon the  congo wetlands forests because these are  key organs of the living being called  gaia that maintain its health and the  reservoirs of biodiversity and the  memory of healthy life on this planet  second priority is regeneration  regenerative agriculture  the soil healing water a reforestation  at forestation to again strengthen the  organs of a living being that's my whole  approach is that earth is alive and then  thirdly on the tissue level to use this  metaphor to to really change the  repeated dousing of the entire biosphere  with chemicals so if we do all these  things conservation and regeneration  that also necessarily includes a vast  reduction in fossil fuel production and  strip mining it's not just the fuels but  but if we were really going to take  seriously to love and care for a living  being  then greenhouse gases are going to have  to fall also so and along those lines  Charles I am getting more and more  inquiries from self-described  environmentalists who ask is the idea of  more nuclear as a bridge to getting off  of fossil fuels something to be taken  seriously where do you fall on that yeah  if you are in the mindset of carbon  reductionism like like equating green  with low carbon then it's pretty hard to  argue against nuclear power I mean you  know there are arguments against it but  but you know it does produce a lot less  emissions than traditional fossil fuel  generation of power but when you look at  a ecosystem living planet view then you  don't want to mess with that stuff  because the damage that the waste and  the potential accidents can cause is is  so horrifying that it's just  inconceivable from a living planet view  that you would use nuclear power so so  in the immediate then what do we now  replace fossil fuel generated energy  with because we can have somewhat of a  reduction maybe in the amount of energy  that that we're producing but we know  that getting people to actually reduce  consumption is a big project in and of  itself in the short-term how do you  start moving off of the fossil fuels and  exactly what do you move to in the  immediate yeah I mean I don't think that  there are any great solutions I mean out  of everything I like rooftop solar for  the best but even more than that is is  conservation and in the if you want to  like take carbon out of the atmosphere  the fastest way to do that  besides disarmament and and you know  dismantling the military industrial  complex the fastest way to do that is  through regenerative agriculture because  soil can absorb enormous amounts of  carbon very very quickly  we learned in school that it takes 500  years to build an inch of topsoil but  there are people out there like like  Ernst gosh in Brazil and Gabe brown and  is it North Dakota or South Dakota  somewhere out there like they're  building an inch a year okay topsoil if  everybody if we did that on a mass scale  it would it would quickly draw down  atmospheric carbon that's I think if if  we're gonna focus on carbon that's what  we should do because also of the side  benefits of repairing the water cycle -  I can go deep deep into the water cycle  but I am aware that we don't have a  whole lot of time well that that in fact  is is all of our time I do want to tell  people that the book is absolutely  fascinating we've been talking with  Charles Eisenstein who is the author of  climate a new story we've only barely  scratched the surface of what he has in  the book and Charles I really appreciate  you joining me today to talk about this  yeah I really enjoyed it as well thank  you so much

